  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nDoes the absence of income tax due imply that an income tax return is not required to be filed?\n\n### Short Answer\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFrom an accounting perspective, entities must prepare and file income tax returns to comply with statutory requirements, even if their taxable income results in no tax liability. This ensures proper documentation and transparency in financial reporting.\n\n### Tax Treatment\nAccording to Section 23 of the NIRC, all citizens and residents are subject to income tax on their worldwide income, and they must file returns to report their income, even if the calculated tax is zero. The filing of a return is a legal obligation that must be fulfilled annually.\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe key difference lies in the obligation to file versus the obligation to pay. While a taxpayer may not owe any tax due to deductions, exemptions, or income levels below the taxable threshold, the requirement to file remains. This distinction is crucial for compliance and avoiding penalties.\n\n### Practical Note\nTaxpayers should ensure they file their income tax returns on time, even if they anticipate no tax due. Failure to file can result in penalties and interest, as the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) mandates compliance with filing requirements irrespective of tax liability.",
